    "content": {
      "component": "blog_article",
      "sharepoint_id": "69",
      "title": "Midwifery Students Bring Hope to Underserved Communities",
      "description": "",
      "date": "Mar 21, 2025",
      "read_time": "3 min read",
      "author": "Brenda Nyamichaba",
      "isGlobal": true,
      "banner_image": {
        "fieldtype": "asset",
        "_local": "69-Banner.jpg"
      },
      "thumbnail": {
        "fieldtype": "asset"
      },
      "languages": [],
      "show_share": true,
      "show_similar": true,
      "similar_show_header": true,
      "category": "",
      "isFeatured": null,
      "body": [
        {
          "component": "rich_text",
          "content": {
            "type": "doc",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "In Dar es Salaam, a reproductive health crisis persists, particularly among adolescents in underserved communities. AKU's School of Nursing and Midwifery, East Africa (SONAM EA) students in Tanzania, as part of their community midwifery course, witnessed these challenges firsthand during home visits. They observed young mothers lacking guidance, male hesitancy in antenatal care, and widespread knowledge gaps."
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "hard_break"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Determined to make a difference, the students, together with the BSc Nursing - Direct Entry class and under the guidance of faculty members Dr Eunice Siaity, Lucy Kisaka, and Dr Stewart Mbelwa, organized a two-day community outreach initiative on February 27 and 28, 2025. "
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "hard_break"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "They visited Saranga Street in Ubungo Municipal and Minondo Street in Kigamboni Municipal, which are known to have rampant reproductive health challenges. Their mission: to provide health care and promote lasting change."
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "hard_break"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "SONAM students led health education sessions, using engaging role plays and demonstrations to break the silence around topics often whispered about but rarely understood. One such session left a lasting impact on a mother of three from Minondo."
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "hard_break"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "\"I used to delay my antenatal care visits, thinking it wasn't urgent until much later in my pregnancy,\" she admitted after watching a skit on the risks of late clinic visits. \"Now, I understand the dangers and the importance of starting early. From now on, I will follow what I've learned and make sure I attend all my appointments.\""
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "hard_break"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "A key thing students hoped to address during this community visit was the breakdown of deep-seated cultural barriers, particularly concerning male involvement in reproductive health. A local street chairman candidly admitted the struggles men face in participating in antenatal care. \"In our community, a man going with his wife to the clinic is seen as strange. Carrying her bag? Sitting beside her as she speaks with a midwife? People will laugh,\" he said with a chuckle. "
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "hard_break"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "As the days unfolded, it became evident that such community visits are much more than a learning experience. Lucy Kisaka, a faculty member at SONAM and a co-facilitator of the Community Midwifery Course, found herself deeply moved by the experience."
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "hard_break"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "\"Guiding and supervising the students in the community was a learning process for me as well,\" she reflected. \"Midwifery in a community setting is more than just clinical skills, it's about understanding social dynamics, breaking cultural barriers, and fostering trust.\""
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "hard_break"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "For the students, the experience was eye-opening. One BScM student, who attended as an invited guest, left with a newfound sense of purpose. \"This experience has shown me the gaps that exist in reproductive health access,\" she said. \"As I prepare for my own community placement next year, I feel more prepared to advocate for better awareness and accessibility.\""
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "hard_break"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "By the end of the community outreach, many members within these communities had received the care they sought, and countless conversations had been sparked. This was a powerful testament of AKU SONAM students, stepping beyond the classroom and textbooks and immersing themselves in the realities of the communities they aspire to serve throughout their nursing journey."
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "hard_break"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
